  "body": {
    "id": "thm:sparse-beyond-birthday",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every array in \\(\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{dense}}\\) with \\(\\rho=0\\), explicitly conditional on the filtration-side premise \\(Q_n/\\sigma_n^2\\xrightarrow{p}1\\) contained in that class,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\widehat\\tau_n-\\tau_n}{\\sqrt{\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}}}\\rightsquigarrow\\mathcal N(0,1)\n\\]\nunder \\(N_n/n\\to0\\), with no condition on \\(N_n^2/n\\).  This is an assumption trade: it replaces the whole-tuple birthday condition in FuSamiiWang2026, Theorems 1--2, by the statistic-specific predictable-variation premise, which is not asserted to follow from bounded potential outcomes.  It includes grouped-unit counts such as \\(N_n\\asymp n^{3/4}\\), for which \\(N_n^2/n\\to\\infty\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "N_n",
      "\\rho",
      "\\tau_n",
      "\\widehat\\tau_n",
      "\\sigma_n^2",
      "Q_n",
      "\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}",
      "\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{dense}}"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:sampling-fraction",
      "ass:scaled-variance-nondegeneracy",
      "ass:predictable-variance-stability",
      "def:pame-estimator",
      "def:cr2-statistic",
      "def:dense-schedule-class",
      "thm:dense-projection-limit",
      "thm:centered-design-clt",
      "thm:cr2-phase-frontier"
    ],
    "justification": "Scalar PAME inference needs a vanishing sampling fraction, not a vanishing whole-tuple collision probability.",
    "gap": "FuSamiiWang2026 imposes the birthday-scale restriction \\(N_n^2/n\\to0\\) because its proof couples the entire group tuple, while this theorem targets only the scalar statistic.",
    "consumer": "Experiments grouping \\(n^{3/4}\\) of \\(n\\) eligible units obtain justified CR2 inference despite failure of whole-design coupling.",
    "proof_tex": "Assume \\(\\rho=0\\).  Theorem~\\ref{thm:dense-projection-limit} then gives\n\\[\n G_n\\sigma_n^2=R_n+o(1),\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nwhereas Theorem~\\ref{thm:cr2-phase-frontier} gives\n\\[\n G_n\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}=R_n+o_p(1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nScaled-variance nondegeneracy and (1) imply \\(R_n\\ge c_\\sigma/2\\) for all sufficiently large \\(n\\).  Dividing (2) by (1), with denominators therefore bounded away from zero after multiplication by \\(G_n\\), yields\n\\[\n \\frac{\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}}{\\sigma_n^2}\\xrightarrow{p}1.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nThe centered Gaussian limit in Theorem~\\ref{thm:centered-design-clt} is conditional on the filtration-side premise \\(Q_n/\\sigma_n^2\\xrightarrow{p}1\\) included in \\(\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{dense}}\\); bounded schedules alone do not supply that premise.  Under it,\n\\[\n \\frac{\\widehat\\tau_n-\\tau_n}{\\sigma_n}\n \\rightsquigarrow\\mathcal N(0,1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nEquation (3) implies that the square root of \\(\\sigma_n^2/\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}\\) converges in probability to one.  The tightness and distribution-function sandwich argument used in the proof of Theorem~\\ref{thm:cr2-phase-frontier}, applied to the product of this square root and the statistic in (4), gives\n\\[\n \\frac{\\widehat\\tau_n-\\tau_n}\n {\\sqrt{\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{CR2},n}}}\n \\rightsquigarrow\\mathcal N(0,1).\n\\]\n\nThe only sampling-fraction step in (1)--(4) is \\(N_n/n\\to0\\), obtained from Assumption~\\ref{ass:sampling-fraction} with \\(\\rho=0\\); no bound on \\(N_n^2/n\\) was used.  To see that this distinction is nonempty, take\n\\[\n N_n=M\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n^{3/4}}{M}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThen \\(N_n\\) is an admissible multiple of the fixed group size, \\(N_n/n=n^{-1/4}(1+o(1))\\to0\\), but \\(N_n^2/n=n^{1/2}(1+o(1))\\to\\infty\\).  Subject to the other stated class conditions, including predictable-variance stability, such a sequence is therefore covered.  Thus the theorem makes an assumption trade for scalar inference; it does not claim that predictable-variance stability follows from boundedness or that the comparator's whole-tuple condition is weakened without replacement.",
